 I do not  think it ever crossed his mind that he  should report him but he should have  done  the second was cannon callin semper he  was a producer in the religious  broadcasting department and worked  closely with several with commendable  honesty when giving evidence to the  review he accepted that he had come to  think that Savile had casual sex with a  lot of girls some of whom might have  been underage he did not discuss what he  knew with his managers because he  thought that they already knew about  Savile and did not seem to be concerned  about it in my view he should have  discussed his concerns with his line  manager I think he now deeply regrets  that he did not the third is Douglas  Muggeridge the controller of Radio one  and two in the early 1970s mr. Mockridge  died many years ago in 1973 he heard  rumors about several sexual activities  and prompted some inquiries  Savile was asked about the rumors and  denied them I think mr. Muggeridge  genuinely believed the rumors to be  untrue however in my view he should have  retained his concerns should have shared  them with colleagues and had a watch  kept on Savile had he done so Savile  might well have been uncovered in the  1970s this was a third missed  opportunity although I have found that  none of several senior managers in  television were aware of his sexual  misconduct  I do criticize them in another important  respect by 1983 there was a good deal of  material in the public domain which  showed Savile in a very bad light  whatever the truth about him this  material showed Savile boasting of past  associations with violent criminals and  proud to be described as having a  prolific  all sex life with young women many  decades younger than himself it is  amazing that it never occurred to  severals managers of the BBC that on  account of this they should not give him  a platform to promote his image as a  good man it is amazing that it never  occurred to them that Savile was not a  suitable role model for the young  audiences of Gemmell Fixit in Manchester  there were two individuals who could  have done something to stop Stewart Hall  Raymond Kali and Tom German Raymond Kali  was the regional television manager in  Manchester from 1970 until 1986 Dame  Linda found that when mr. Kali took up  his post he challenged Stuart Hall about  rumours that he was having sex with  women in his dressing room these rumors  did not suggest that these were underage  girls Mr Colley warned Stuart Hall as to  his future conduct but he did not follow  up his warning he assumed that Stuart  Hall would desist he overestimated his  own authority and he underestimated  Stuart Hall's duplicity had mr. Kali  kept a watch on Hall it is likely that  he would have been stopped this was the  first missed opportunity in respect of  Stuart Hall the fourth overall mr.  German was the news editor in Manchester  during the 1970s and 80s he is dead  Dame Linda has concluded that he was  probably aware that Stuart Hall was  involved in inappropriate sexual conduct  on BBC premises if he was it seems to me  that he should have shared his concerns  with mr. Kali he did not do so and this  may well have been the fifth missed  opportunity this report makes sorry  reading for the BBC  Dame Linda and I have found disturbing  things and have not hesitated to expose  them from a conversation I had with Lord  Hall on Tuesday after he had read my  report I am satisfied that he entirely  accepts but there is much work ahead for  him the BBC Trust and the Executive  Board if the BBC is to regain the  confidence of the public in respect of  its culture and its child protection  practices I wish now to speak about the  victims I must record my annoyance at  the complete wreck  disregard of their welfare shown by a  website that chose to publish parts of  an early draft of my report this  unexpected and selective publication was  highly distressing for them and had no  public interest justification whatsoever  I want to thank every witness who gave  evidence to me enter Dame Linda  but above all I want to send a personal  message to the victims of Seville and  Stuart Hall many of whom will be  listening thank you for your  contribution and your courage some of  you have told me and Dame Linda that you  blame yourselves for what happened and  for not reporting it at the time you are  not to blame and your reasons for not  reporting are wholly understandable I  know that it was very difficult for you  to speak to the review you did so in  order to help us understand what had  happened and why it had not been  discovered society owes you a great debt  your legacy is that you have helped to  convince us all of the importance of  ensuring that young and vulnerable  people have the confidence to report  abuse and that when they do so their  voices will be heard  and treated with the same respect as  those who hold power in our society I  think that is a legacy of which you can  be justifiably proud thank you
 

